        "title": "The functions of language",
        "text": "The question 'Why do we use language?' hardly seems to need an answer. But because we are so familiar with speech and writing in everyday life, it can be difficult to realise how complex the skills we have learned are. This is particularly true when we try to define the range of functions that language can be used for.\n\n'To communicate our ideas' is the answer most of us would give to the question – and this must surely be the most widely recognised function of language. Whenever we tell people about ourselves or our circumstances, or ask for information about other people and their circumstances, we are using language to exchange facts and opinions. It is the kind of language found in any spoken or written interaction where people want to learn from each other. But it would be wrong to think of it as the only way in which we use language. There are several other functions where the communication of ideas is irrelevant.\n\n### Emotional expression\nMr X carefully leans his walking stick against a wall, but it falls over. He tries again, and it falls a second time. He shouts at the walking stick. How should we classify this function of language? It cannot be 'communication of ideas', because there is no one else in the room.\n\nHere we have one of the commonest uses of language: a way of getting rid of nervous energy when we are under stress. This type of language can be used whether or not we are alone. Swear words are probably the commonest signals used in this way, especially when we are angry. But there are also many words that we use to express positive feelings, such as affection, or our reaction to beautiful art or scenery.\n\nThe most common linguistic expressions of emotion are conventional words or phrases (such as 'Gosh' and 'My!') and the semi-linguistic noises often called interjections (such as 'Wow' and 'Ouch').\n\nMrs P sneezes violently. Mrs Q says, 'Bless you!' Mrs P says, 'Thank you.' Again, this hardly seems to be language being used to communicate ideas; rather, it is used to maintain a comfortable relationship between people. No factual content is involved. Similarly, phrases such as 'Good morning' or 'Pleased to meet you', and the usual exchanges about health or the weather, do not 'communicate ideas' in the usual sense. Phrases of this type often state the obvious (for example, 'It's a lovely day') or have no content at all (for example, 'Hello'). They are used to maintain a friendly relationship between people, and they come from the basic human need to signal such an attitude. If someone does not say these things when they are expected to, their absence may be seen as a sign of distance or even danger.\n\nThese examples apply to English and many European languages. But cultures vary greatly in the topics they allow in this type of social interaction. The weather is not as universal a conversation-filler as English people might like to think! Other topics are used for the same purpose, and some cultures avoid this type of language and prefer silence.\n\n### The power of sound\nIn many situations, the only obvious reason for using language is the enjoyment that the users or listeners get from the sounds, and this applies to all age groups. Many children's rhymes are of this type, as are the words of popular songs and the voices of people singing in the kitchen or the bath."
